WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.880
In this course, we have established that the&nbsp;
BETWEEN keyword is used to set criteria to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.160
return records between two numbers or two dates.&nbsp;
Sometimes, though, we just want to see something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.920
greater than or less than a number or something&nbsp;
that occurs before or after a certain date. Take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.360
for example, this query that shows orders placed&nbsp;
in a certain month. What if I just want to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.480
orders that happened after a certain date. I will&nbsp;
change the WHERE clause to use a greater than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:34.240
symbol (&gt;), one of several comparison operators,&nbsp;
to show orders that occurred after June 1, 2014.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:41.360
I will execute this, and we get&nbsp;
just over 900 rows. One real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.360
aspect of comparisons to consider is this: If&nbsp;
someone says, get me everything after June 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.400
does the person really mean that? Or, should June&nbsp;
1 be included? Sometimes, you will need to clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.080
those situations. Right now, our results&nbsp;
do not show anything on June 1 because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.120
indicated we wanted to see activity from after&nbsp;
that date. However, if I change the comparison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:13.360
operator to greater than or equal to (&gt;=) and then&nbsp;
execute this again, we get the orders from June 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.840
Let's use the SubTotal field to see the less than&nbsp;
(&lt;) and less than or equal to (&lt;=) comparisons at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:28.880
work. I will add AND Subtotal &lt; 159 to this&nbsp;
criteria. We do not need single quotes around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:37.120
this amount because this is a numeric value. I&nbsp;
will execute this query, and we do lose a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:46.000
records. Let's see what happens when I change&nbsp;
the criteria to &lt;= and execute this again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:55.760
We see three more records as three orders have a&nbsp;
subtotal of exactly 159 dollars. The big takeaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.720
with comparison operators isn't how to use&nbsp;
these operators; it is making sure we understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:07.280
exactly what someone wants in situations&nbsp;
in which comparison operators can be used.

